# 28. DEFINITION OF DONE

The project is not complete merely because the website looks good.

A feature is complete when its user interface, backend behavior, validation, permissions, persistence, and error handling work together.

Before finishing:

1. Verify that the application starts.
2. Verify that the frontend builds.
3. Verify that the backend starts.
4. Verify that the database schema and migrations are valid.
5. Verify that users can authenticate.
6. Verify that roles and permissions are enforced on the server.
7. Verify that AI generation uses the configured provider.
8. Verify that generated lessons can be saved.
9. Verify that PDF, Word, and PowerPoint exports work.
10. Verify that plan entitlements are enforced.
11. Verify that subscription activation requires trusted payment confirmation.
12. Verify that scheduling prevents conflicts and capacity violations.
13. Verify that users cannot access unauthorized student records.
14. Verify that missing integrations produce clear, honest errors.
15. Verify that no secrets are exposed to the frontend.
16. Verify that existing working features have not been accidentally removed.

After implementing the changes, provide a concise implementation report containing:

- What was implemented.
- Which existing features were preserved.
- Which files were added or changed.
- Which database migrations were created.
- Which tests were run and their actual results.
- Which environment variables must be configured.
- Which external services require accounts or credentials.
- Which features are fully working.
- Which features remain incomplete and why.
- The exact commands needed to run the application.

Do not describe a feature as complete if it is only a placeholder.

